{{/*
string_content_check emits the content vocabulary's assertion against a value
already known to be a Go string.

There is one arm, dispatching to the one shared helper, for the same reason
string_format_check has one: every position that asserts contentEncoding or
contentMediaType comes through here -- a struct field, an alias over its own
value, and the wrapper a content keyword with no declared "type" resolves to --
so the three cannot disagree about what the keywords mean.

.Expr      a Go expression of type string
.Path      what to name the value in the error message ("value", a JSON pointer, ...)
.Args      the argument list a Path carrying format verbs needs, or empty
.Encoding  the contentEncoding to decode by, or empty
.MediaType the contentMediaType to parse the decoded bytes as, or empty

A rule with neither keyword is never built, so there is no empty-call case to
guard: contentCheckFor refuses to produce one.
*/}}
{{define "string_content_check"}}
if _err := schemagenContentString({{.Expr}}, {{printf "%q" .Encoding}}, {{printf "%q" .MediaType}}); _err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("{{.Path}}: %w", {{if .Args}}{{.Args}}, {{end}}_err)
}
{{- end}}
